\begin{abstract}
We report four boundary conditions and extensions of Silent Discordance, a recently discovered quantum many-body diagnostic phenomenon defined by the structural contrast between a smooth coarse-grained entity diagnosis and a non-monotonic fine-grained relational diagnosis. First, using a corrected isotropic Heisenberg Hamiltonian, we show that the enhanced diagnosis is \emph{exactly invariant} under the addition of a Rashba spin-orbit coupling term ($D=0.3$) that breaks SU(2) symmetry while preserving both spin-flip and diagonal channels; the two diagnostics agree to machine precision at every non-degenerate point. This exactness corrects an earlier report of a scaling constant ($\approx0.958$), which we trace to a Hamiltonian construction error in one analysis script that effectively halved the XY weight and produced an XXZ model. Second, a complete even--odd chain analysis ($N=4$--$21$) reveals a perfect parity separation in TAT-7 triadic resonance: every even chain resonates ($p \le 0.026$), every odd chain is silent ($p \ge 0.69$). This decisively rules out divisibility by 3 as a confound and generalizes the previously reported ``prime-chain silence'' to a law-grade \emph{Sultanov Silence}. Third, a high-resolution scan (301 points) on acyclic tree graphs reveals a V-shaped valley that was masked by low resolution in the initial experiments; a falsification test on five tree topologies with three random seeds each (15 runs, 100\% valley presence) transforms the first law of Silent Discordance from a binary ``cycle necessity'' to a quantitative ``cycle enhancement''. Fourth, Silent Discordance survives in a non-equilibrium steady state under a finite spin bias, demonstrating that the phenomenon is not tied to thermal equilibrium. We conclude with a unifying observation: asymmetric injection of contradiction is the common necessary condition across all extensions, suggesting a potential seventh law that remains open for independent verification. All data, scripts, and protocols are publicly available under the same Zenodo archive as the primary discovery paper.
\end{abstract}

