\section{Introduction}

In a companion paper~\cite{Li2026Primary}, we reported the systematic discovery of Silent Discordance, a novel quantum many-body diagnostic phenomenon defined by a structural contrast: within the same parameter interval, a coarse-grained entity diagnosis (mean magnetization or mean correlation strength) remains smooth, while a fine-grained relational diagnosis (standard deviation of entanglement spectrum or edge correlation fluctuations) exhibits a pronounced non-monotonic extremum at a specific location. The primary paper established three laws governing the emergence of this phenomenon---law of cycle enhancement, law of fully symmetric interaction necessity, and law of morphology-structure correspondence---and described six distinct morphologies across seven relation graph topologies and four generation rules.

The present paper reports four boundary conditions and extensions that emerged during the refinement and independent verification of the primary discoveries. Each extension was motivated by a specific unresolved question from the primary work:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Rashba SOC invariance:} Does the phenomenon survive under an interaction that breaks SU(2) symmetry while preserving spin-flip channels? If so, is the survival exact or approximate?
\item \textbf{Parity separation:} Does the TAT-7 triadic resonance, which was independently verified as a structural diagnostic, exhibit a systematic dependence on the parity of the chain length? If so, is the relevant variable primality, divisibility by 3, or parity itself?
\item \textbf{Acyclic valley emergence:} Is the absence of Silent Discordance in tree graphs a genuine physical boundary, or an artifact of low scan resolution? If a valley exists in acyclic structures, how does its stability compare to cyclic structures?
\item \textbf{Non-equilibrium survival:} Does Silent Discordance persist away from thermal equilibrium, under a finite spin bias?
\end{enumerate}

Throughout this paper, we strictly adhere to the five-axiom framework of the Crystal Vein Philosophy~\cite{Li2026Relation}: Relation Ontology, Contradiction Dynamics, Practical Intervention, Resonance Tuning, and Meta-Reflection. In particular, we predefine success criteria before each experiment, report all negative results with the same rigor as positive ones, and explicitly declare the limitations of every conclusion. We are also committed to the principle of ``opposing alignment'': we do not retrospectively match numerical results to theoretical expectations, and we report deviations from any proposed law with the same prominence as confirmations.

The structure of this paper is as follows. Section~II reports the exact invariance under Rashba SOC, including the complete history of the Hamiltonian construction error and its correction. Section~III reports the complete parity analysis and the generalization to Sultanov Silence. Section~IV reports the high-resolution tree-graph scans and the refinement of the first law. Section~V reports the non-equilibrium survival. Section~VI reports the current algebra model experiment and the unifying observation of asymmetric driving necessity. Section~VII summarizes the boundary conditions and lists the open questions. An appendix provides the detailed collaborative methodology.

\section{Exact Invariance under Rashba SOC}
\label{sec:soc}

\subsection{Motivation and experimental design}

The second law of Silent Discordance states that the interaction must contain both spin-flip terms ($XX+YY$) and a diagonal term ($ZZ$). This condition is satisfied by the isotropic Heisenberg model, but it does not require SU(2) symmetry. A natural test of the law's precise formulation is to add a Rashba spin-orbit coupling (SOC) term, which breaks SU(2) symmetry while preserving both required channels.

The Rashba SOC term we consider has the Dzyaloshinskii--Moriya (DM) form with the DM vector along the $y$-axis:
\begin{equation}
H_{\text{SOC}} = D \sum_{j} (\sigma_j^z \sigma_{j+1}^x - \sigma_j^x \sigma_{j+1}^z),
\end{equation}
with $D=0.3$. This term is added to the isotropic Heisenberg Hamiltonian:
\begin{equation}
H = \sum_{j} (\sigma_j^x \sigma_{j+1}^x + \sigma_j^y \sigma_{j+1}^y + \sigma_j^z \sigma_{j+1}^z) + H_{\text{SOC}}.
\end{equation}

The experiment is designed as follows. On a chain of $N=6$ sites with a contradiction edge at the center bond $(2,3)$, we scan the contradiction strength $s$ from $0$ to $3.0$ and compute the enhanced diagnosis $F(s)$---the standard deviation of nearest-neighbor $\langle \sigma^z \sigma^z \rangle$ correlations---for both the pure Heisenberg case ($D=0$) and the SOC case ($D=0.3$). The predefined success criterion is: if the enhanced diagnosis under SOC differs from the pure Heisenberg case by less than the numerical precision of the exact diagonalization method ($\sim10^{-6}$), we declare \emph{exact invariance}; if the difference is finite but the morphology is preserved, we declare \emph{robust survival}; if the valley disappears, we declare \emph{breaking}.

\subsection{Corrected Hamiltonian and exact invariance}
\label{sec:soc_corrected}

The initial analysis of this experiment was performed with a Hamiltonian construction script that contained an error: the off-diagonal matrix element of the XX+YY terms was set to half its correct value. This produced an XXZ model with XY weight equal to half the ZZ weight, rather than the isotropic Heisenberg model. Under this incorrect Hamiltonian, the enhanced diagnosis under SOC appeared to be related to the pure Heisenberg case by a nearly constant ratio of $\approx0.958$, which was initially interpreted as a scaling relation.

Rastislav Draho\v{s} identified this error during a line-by-line audit of the two analysis scripts. The corrected Hamiltonian, constructed using Pauli matrices with the correct off-diagonal weight, yields a dramatically different result: the enhanced diagnosis under SOC is \emph{identically equal} to the pure Heisenberg case at every non-degenerate point.

