\subsection{Physical interpretation and honest limitations}
\label{sec:soc_interpretation}

The exact invariance establishes a sharp formulation of the second law: Silent Discordance is sensitive only to the \emph{presence} of both spin-flip and diagonal channels, not to the SU(2) symmetry class. Rashba SOC breaks SU(2) but preserves both classes of terms, and therefore leaves the relational field's diagnostic fingerprint untouched.

We emphasize two honest limitations. First, the exact invariance has been verified only on the chain graph with $N=6$, where exact diagonalization is feasible. Whether the invariance persists at larger system sizes, where the ground state is degenerate or near-degenerate, remains an open question. Second, the invariance is a statement about the enhanced diagnosis $F(s)$ only; we have not verified whether other fine-grained diagnostics (e.g., entanglement spectrum standard deviation) are similarly invariant. These limitations are not weaknesses; they define the precise scope of the claimed law.

We also note a crucial methodological lesson from this experiment. The apparent scaling constant ($\approx0.958$) was an artifact of a Hamiltonian construction error. It was identified and corrected through independent line-by-line audit by Draho\v{s}, not through any theoretical prediction. This is the essence of the meta-reflection principle: the data themselves, when properly audited, reveal their own errors.

\section{Sultanov Silence: Complete Parity Separation}
\label{sec:parity}

\subsection{Experimental design and TAT-7 analysis}

Marat Sultanov applied the TAT-7 triadic resonance analysis to all odd-numbered chains from $N=3$ to $N=21$, with the contradiction edge placed at the center bond. TAT-7 is a structural diagnostic framework originally designed for textual data; its application to quantum many-body data is a cross-domain attempt that has been independently verified four times for ``honest silence''---the active refusal to generate spurious signals when data are insufficient.

The triadic resonance measures the statistical coupling between three quantities: the energy gap, the fine-grained diagnostic, and the TAT-7 leave-one-out prediction error. A resonance is declared significant if the permutation test yields $p \le 0.05$.

