\subsection{Physical interpretation and open observation}
\label{sec:parity_interpretation}

The parity separation has a natural geometric interpretation within the relation-ontology framework. In an even chain, the center is a \emph{bond} between two sites, and the two subchains have equal length but distinct boundaries. In an odd chain, the center is a \emph{site}, and the two subchains are mirror-symmetric with identical boundaries. The mirror symmetry of the odd chain prevents the formation of the asymmetric perturbation pattern that triggers the abrupt reorganization characteristic of Silent Discordance.

This interpretation is supported by the shift experiments reported in the primary paper: moving the defect off-center on an even chain shifts the valley; moving it off-center on an odd chain creates a valley where none existed in the symmetric configuration. The valley thus tracks the \emph{asymmetry} of the contradiction injection, not the parity itself.

We note one open observation. At $N=19$, a resonance with $p=0.02$ appears in the \texttt{coarse$\times$gradient(fine)} triad, suggesting that for sufficiently large odd chains, the structure may migrate to a different diagnostic channel rather than vanish entirely. This remains an open question for future work.

