\subsection{Honest limitations}
\label{sec:non_eq_limits}

We note three limitations. First, the non-equilibrium steady state is constructed from a finite chain with a magnetic field gradient; it is not a true open quantum system with dissipation. Second, the valley position on the 13-point grid ($s=0.38$) differs from the high-resolution equilibrium value ($s\approx0.10$); this may reflect either a physical difference between equilibrium and non-equilibrium, or a grid-resolution artifact. A high-resolution non-equilibrium scan has not yet been performed. Third, the survival has been verified only on the chain topology; whether it extends to other topologies remains open.

\section{Current Algebra Model: Asymmetric Driving Triggers Silent Discordance}
\label{sec:current_algebra}

\subsection{Motivation}
\label{sec:ca_motivation}

Inspired by the recent current algebra framework of Bai and Phillips~\cite{BaiPhillips2026}, which demonstrates that strongly correlated systems can be formulated in terms of local currents obeying an $\mathfrak{su}_1(2)$ affine Lie algebra, we constructed a controlled experiment to test a unifying hypothesis emerging from the shift experiments and the non-equilibrium results: \emph{Symmetric driving suppresses Silent Discordance; asymmetric driving triggers it.}

\subsection{Experimental design}
\label{sec:ca_design}

We consider a Heisenberg chain of $N=6$ sites under a Zeeman field. Two configurations are compared:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Uniform field:} All sites experience the same Zeeman field $h_z$.
\item \textbf{Asymmetric field:} The left half experiences $+h_z$, the right half experiences $-h_z$.
\end{itemize}

We scan $h_z$ from $0$ to $3.0$ and compute the enhanced diagnosis.

