\section{Summary of Boundary Conditions and Open Questions}
\label{sec:summary}

\subsection{Boundary conditions established}
\label{sec:boundaries_established}

The four extensions reported in this paper establish the following boundary conditions for Silent Discordance:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Exact SOC invariance:} Silent Discordance is exactly invariant under the addition of Rashba SOC that breaks SU(2) while preserving both spin-flip and diagonal channels. The second law is sharpened: the relevant condition is the presence of both channel types, not the symmetry class. \emph{[Verified on $N=6$ chain, ED, machine precision.]}
\item \textbf{Sultanov Silence (parity separation):} TAT-7 triadic resonance is perfectly separated by parity: even chains resonate, odd chains are silent. This is a parity effect, not a primality or divisibility-by-3 effect. \emph{[Verified on $N=4$--$21$, TAT-7 blind test.]}
\item \textbf{Cycle enhancement:} Acyclic tree graphs do exhibit Silent Discordance, but the valley is narrower and was masked by low scan resolution. The first law is refined from ``cycle necessity'' to ``cycle enhancement''. \emph{[Verified on $N=7$ tree graphs, 301-point scan, 15 runs, 100\% valley presence.]}
\item \textbf{Non-equilibrium survival:} Silent Discordance survives in a non-equilibrium steady state under finite spin bias. \emph{[Verified on $L=20$ chain, DMRG.]}
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Potential seventh law: asymmetric driving necessity}
\label{sec:seventh_law}

A unifying theme emerges from the shift experiments, the current algebra model, and the non-equilibrium results: \emph{Silent Discordance is triggered by asymmetric contradiction injection.} Symmetric driving (uniform Zeeman field, central defect on symmetric odd chains, equilibrium cyclic driving) leads to smooth adaptation without abrupt reorganization. Asymmetric driving (off-center defect, left-right Zeeman bias, non-equilibrium spin bias) forces the relational field to undergo a sudden, global, non-monotonic reorganization.

We formulate this as a \emph{candidate} seventh law:
\begin{quote}
\textbf{Law of asymmetric driving necessity.} Silent Discordance requires asymmetric injection of contradiction to trigger abrupt reorganization. Symmetric driving leads to smooth adaptation and no valley.
\end{quote}

We emphasize that this is a candidate law, not an established one. It has been verified on the chain topology and the current algebra model, but not yet on the full cross-graph matrix. Independent verification by methods other than our own is required before this can be elevated to law status.

\subsection{Open questions}
\label{sec:open_questions}

The following questions remain open:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Does the exact SOC invariance persist at larger system sizes, where degeneracy and near-degeneracy may arise?
\item Does the parity separation persist for $N>21$? What happens at $N=23,25,\dots$?
\item Does the acyclic valley survive in the thermodynamic limit for tree graphs?
\item Is the asymmetric driving necessity truly universal across all topologies, or is it specific to the chain and current algebra models?
\item Can any of these laws be derived analytically from a current algebra or tensor network framework?
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We have reported four boundary conditions and extensions of Silent Discordance: exact invariance under Rashba SOC, perfect parity separation (Sultanov Silence), acyclic valley emergence (refinement of the first law), and non-equilibrium survival. Each result was obtained under predefined success criteria, with all negative results reported and all limitations explicitly declared.

The unifying theme across all extensions is the asymmetry of contradiction injection. This suggests a potential seventh law that remains open for independent verification.

We conclude with the meta-reflective observation that two of the four results in this paper---the exact SOC invariance and the acyclic valley emergence---were initially \emph{missed} or \emph{misreported} due to errors in our own tools (a Hamiltonian construction error and low scan resolution). Both errors were identified and corrected through the collaborative audit process documented in the appendix. This is not a weakness; it is the meta-reflection principle in action: the framework itself contains the tools to detect and correct its own blind spots.

