\section{Appendix: Collaborative Audit Methodology}
\label{app:collaboration}

This appendix provides the detailed record of the collaborative audit that led to the corrections reported in this paper. It demonstrates how the tool-rationality paradox was rigorously prevented at every key juncture.

\subsection{Timeline of the Hamiltonian error identification}

The SOC experiment was originally analyzed with a script that constructed the Hamiltonian as follows:
\begin{verbatim}
H[state, flipped] += w   # off-diagonal element weight = 1
\end{verbatim}
This construction sets the off-diagonal matrix element of the XX+YY terms to unity. The correct construction, using Pauli matrices, gives:
\begin{verbatim}
H += w * (to_full(sx,i) @ to_full(sx,j)).real
H += w * (to_full(sy,i) @ to_full(sy,j)).real
\end{verbatim}
which sets the off-diagonal element to 2. The ratio of XY weight to ZZ weight is therefore 1/2 in the incorrect script, producing an XXZ model, versus 1 in the correct script, producing the isotropic Heisenberg model.

Rastislav Draho\v{s} identified this discrepancy by comparing the two scripts line by line, computing the ground-state spectra for a $N=4$ chain, and showing that they differed:
\begin{verbatim}
Pauli   ground -6.464102   first excited -3.828427
direct  ground -4.472136   first excited -3.236068
\end{verbatim}
This discrepancy is too large to be numerical noise, and directly demonstrates that the two scripts solve different Hamiltonians.

\subsection{Timeline of the DM gauge factor self-correction}

Draho\v{s} initially proposed that the SOC ratio could be explained by a DM gauge factor:
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{1+D^2}},
\end{equation}
citing Shekhtman--Entin-Wohlman--Aharony (SEA, 1992)~\cite{SEA1992}. He then corrected himself, pointing out that the argument was a category error: a rescaling of the Hamiltonian cannot change a dimensionless ground-state correlation function. The exact invariance under the corrected Hamiltonian confirmed this self-correction.

\subsection{Timeline of the tree-graph high-resolution scan}

The initial low-resolution scan (9--13 points) on the tree graph was reported as a negative result. The meta-reflection principle required us to ask whether our own tool (the scan grid) might be too coarse to resolve a narrow signal. The high-resolution scan (301 points) confirmed this: a valley existed, but its width was smaller than the grid spacing of the original scan. This is an example of a tool-induced false negative, and the correction is recorded here for full transparency.

